{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And the name of the Israelite man who was struck, who was struck.</b> Rashi explained: in the place where Scripture traces the lineage of the righteous for praise, it traces the lineage of the wicked for disgrace. But what disgrace is this, that it says 'Zimri son of Salu'? If for praise, well and good — for Elazar and Aharon were known in the gates for name and glory; but this Salu — who is he and what is his name, that you should know it is for disgrace? What taint is heard about him? And further: what is written, 'who was struck, who was struck' — why does it duplicate his wounds twice? It should have said: 'and the name of the man who struck the Midianite woman,' etc. And moreover it should have said: 'and an Israelite man who was struck — his name was Zimri.' But since it says 'and the name of the Israelite man who was struck, who was struck,' infer from it that 'who was struck' refers back to the person himself, but 'who was struck' (the first one) refers back to the name — to say that his name was 'struck,' i.e., disgraced." "And it appears that from the force of these difficulties did Rabbi Yohanan derive his teaching in tractate Sanhedrin (82b): he had five names: Zimri, because he became like a tossed egg; Salu, because he concealed the sin of his family. For one must indeed wonder what Rabbi Yohanan saw to expound all these names for disgrace; rather, he too expounded 'and the name of the man who was struck' — that the word 'who was struck' refers back to the name: for even though they were not originally named for the deed, rather his sins caused that they investigated his name and the name of his fathers and interpreted them after the event — to tell you that 'the name of the wicked rots' (Proverbs 10:7). And his proof from the Torah is what is written: 'and the name of the Israelite man who was struck' — the name was struck, while 'who was struck the Midianite woman' refers to the person himself. And likewise, 'and the name of the woman who was struck, Cozbi' — as the Gemara concludes: Cozbi, because she was false (כִּזְּבָה) with her father, for her name too was 'struck' in order to expound it for disgrace. And if you want to say that 'who was struck' refers to the man, while 'who was struck the Midianite woman' refers to the name — one may say that this is why it says 'אֶת the Midianite woman': because he was 'with' (אֵת) the Midianite woman and engaged in many acts of intercourse until he became like a tossed egg; therefore he became strange (מוּזָר) to his brothers like a tossed egg (מֻזֶרֶת), and they expounded the name Zimri after the event." "And there are those who say that this is why it says 'אֶת the Midianite woman' — that he was literally with her, for had Zimri withdrawn, he would not have been liable to be killed. And there are those who say that even though he was struck in this world, nevertheless he did not escape being 'with' (אֵת) the Midianite woman literally in the World to Come, in the manner that is said (Genesis 39:10): 'to lie beside her' — in this world; 'to be with her' — in the World to Come (Yoma 35b)." "And besides what our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 82b) expounded regarding these names for disgrace — for Elohim has interpretations aplenty — I too will answer my share to interpret them in another way. For we find our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sotah 22b) concerning the hypocrites (חַנֵפִים) who do the deed of Zimri and seek reward like Pinhas — now was Zimri among the hypocrites? Was he not an openly wicked man? Rather, it is certainly from the fact that he said to Moses, 'Is this one forbidden or permitted?' — infer from it that he acted like dyed hypocrites (צְבוּעִים), to legitimize his deeds, to say that what he did was according to the law. And since our Rabbis of blessed memory held him to be a חָנֵף, one may expound all his names on this pattern: for every חָנֵף and hypocrite always has his praise on his lips to praise himself — and 'Zimri' is a term of praise, for 'עָזִּי וְזִמְרָת יָהּ' (Exodus 15:2), its Targum is 'my strength and my praise.' And the name 'Salu' too is a term of praise, from the language of 'סֹלּוּ לָרוֹכֵב בָּעֲרָבוֹת' (Psalms 68:5). And likewise 'Cozbi,' for every חָנֵף is a liar (כּוֹזֵב), for his mouth and his heart are not equal; and 'daughter of Zur,' for they elevate themselves to sit in a high stronghold (צוּר). And regarding both of them it mentioned 'head' and 'prince,' for this is the very thing that the soul of the hypocrites seeks. Therefore it says 'who was struck אֶת the Midianite woman' — for the names of both of them were struck equally, being expounded upon a single theme."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The verse citation 'לִשְׁכַּב אֶצְלָהּ ... לִהְיוֹת עִמָּהּ' is derived from Genesis 39:10 and Yoma 35b's derashah on that verse, though the Kli Yakar labels it only with the Yoma reference."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:15.301227320Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}